## 1. Introduction
Hydrogen sulfide $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}\right)$ , as well-known a toxic and hazardous gas, usually occurs in crude petroleum, or results from the microbial breakdown of organic matter in the absence of oxygen gas, such as in swamps and sewers system [1]. Generally, 10 ppm is its threshold limit value, and only allowing 8 h exposure value. When the concentration of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ gas is more than the critical concentration $(100\mathsf{p p m})$ , human’s nervous system is usually impacted, while above 250 ppm can directly lead to death [2–4]. Thus, development of gas sensors with satisfactory capabilities for determination of hydrogen sulfide is intensively driven by the ever-expanding monitoring needs in various environmental settings.
To date, so many gas sensors for real-time monitoring hydrogen sulfide have been established with different sensing principles, including semiconducting metal-oxide sensors [5,6], electrochemical sensors [7], optical sensors [8], conducting-polymer sensors [9] and piezeoelectric sensors [10]. It is worth mentioning here that cataluminescence (CTL), a novel chemiluminescence (CL) technique, is one of promising transduction principles for gas sensing. Usually, detected substances are catalytic oxidized (by $0_{2}$ in air) to yield intensive CTL, when it gets through the surface of catalysts [11]. Owing to the simple device, reproducibility, highly stability and selectivity [12], CTL-based $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ sensor have attracted much attention [13–16]. Since 2004, Zhang et al. [17] found that $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ gas was catalytic oxidized at the surface of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ by ${{0}_{2}}$ in air to generate CL. Our group has also done a series of studies on $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ CTL sensors [18–22]. Among them, hierarchical hollow microsphere and flower-like $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ displayed high efficiency and selectivity for $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ gas sensing with the detection limitation of $0.5\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , while the response and recovery time were 5 s and 25 s, respectively, which was quite a bit longer [18]. To further shorten the detection time, enclosed hollow tubular ZnO was also synthesized as $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ sensor, which made both the response and recovery time reduced $90\%$ , but the limit of detection increased to $7\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ [19]. It may be caused by the limited porosity and specific surface area which can’t provide more active sites. Wan et al. [20] have synthesized four MOFs to quickly and effectively detect $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ . However, it is worth noting that both MIL-101(Cr) and MIL-100(Fe) were of poor thermal stability, and they would completely collapsed after the detection of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ . Thus, fabricating a novel method to synthesize materials with high stability, high specific surface area and high porosity for fast and sensitive $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ sensing is still a challenge.
Transition metal oxides have drawn extensive attention in the field of gas sensing. Compared with noble metals, they are earth-abundant, stabilized and economic-friendly [23]. Meanwhile,
Spinel-type cobalt oxide $({\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4})$ , a transition metals oxides, have widely applied as sensor material based on its naturally unique structure and two kinds of different valence of cations [24,25]. For example, F.J. Stadler et al. [26] have developed a chemical bath deposition route to synthesize dandelion flower-like $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ as electrical conductivity sensing material for $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ detection. To our knowledge, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ has not been used as cataluminescent $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ sensor. As a consequence, we try to explore a novel method to synthesize high porosity and high specific surface area $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ as CTL-based sensing material.
In recent years, many efforts focus on using the MOFs as templates or precursors to synthesize porous nanostructure metal oxides, metal oxide decorated carbons and porous carbon materials by controlling thermolysis conditions [27]. For example, Hoi Ri Moon et al. [28] utilized Mg-MOF as precursor to prepare tunable nanoporous MgO, which exhibited outstanding $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption capacity. Il-Doo Kim et al. [29] used ZIF-8, Pd metal and ${\mathsf{W O}}_{3}$ as precursors to obtain cube-like composite for toluene sensor. All these efforts have demonstrated that MOFs-derived nanomaterials can keep the primary features of MOFs, such as high specific surface area and controllable porosity [30–34]. And porosity can provide more active sites as well as have a major affection on the performance of catalysis [27]. Therefore, we believe that $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ synthesized via thermal decomposition of MOFs can be used as a CTL sensor material to improve selectivity and catalytic performance.
Herein, we report a facile method to obtain $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ with variable shapes and sizes by thermal decomposition of different cobaltbased MOFs including dodecahedra ZIF-67, nanosheets packed Co-MOF-71 and nanoplate Co-BTC, then these three cobalt-based MOFs and corresponding derivatives $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ were used in CTL-based sensor to detect $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ . Fortunately, the results were desirable. In addition, the impact parameters on the experimental process were also discussed in detail.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Chemicals
All chemicals used in this work were of analytical grade and used without any pretreatment. Cobalt chloride tetrahydrate and cobaltous nitrate hexahydrate were purchased from Chengdu Kelong Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd (Chengdu, China). 1,4- benzenedicarboxylic acid and 2-methylimidazol were purchased from Aladdin Chemistry Co. Ltd. (Shanghai, China). 1,3,5-benzene tricarboxylic acid was purchased from Chengdu Best Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd (Chengdu, China). N,N-dimethylformamide and methanol were purchased from Kermel Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. (Tianjin, China). Ethanol was purchased from Chengdu Jinshan Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd (Chengdu, China). Water $(18.24\mathrm{M}\Omega\mathrm{cm})$
used in this whole work was obtained from a water purification system (ULUOURE, Chengdu, China).
## 2.2. Synthesis of Co-MOFs
Dodecahedra ZIF-67 was synthesized according to a literature method [23]. $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\left(1.455\mathrm{g}\right)$ and 2-methylimidazole $(1.642\mathrm{g})$ were dissolved in $40\mathrm{mL}$ methanol and $40\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol, respectively. Then the above two kinds of solutions were mixed together and stirred for 1 min until these reactants dissolving thoroughly. Next the violet solution was kept at room temperature for $24\mathrm{h}$ . Last but one the violet precipitates were collected by centrifuging and washing with ethanol. Finally, the violet powder was obtained after drying in vacuum.
Nanosheets packed Co-MOF-71 was synthesized following a previously reported method [35]. Mixed solution containing $48\mathrm{mL}$ DMF and $12\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol was prepared. And $0.75\mathrm{g}\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $0.428{\mathrm{g}}$ terephthalic acid were added into the above solution which was stirred until these reactants were dissolved thoroughly. Subsequently, the mixture was transferred into a polytetrafluoroethylene (Teflon)-lined autoclave and maintained at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . After cooling down to room temperature, the purple products were collected by centrifuging and washing with DMF and ethanol. Lastly, the purple powder was obtained after drying in vacuum.
Nanoplate Co-BTC was synthesized according to the previous method reported by our group [36]. $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ and ${\mathsf{C o C l}}_{2}{\cdot}4{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ were dissolved into ethanol solution and aqueous solution, respectively. Then $4\mathrm{mL}$ $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ solution $(0.210\mathrm{g},1\mathrm{mM})$ and $10\mathrm{mL}$ ${\mathsf{C o C l}}_{2}{\cdot}4{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ solution $(0.4134\mathrm{g}$ , $1.66\mathrm{mM}$ ) were rapidly mixed and moved in a $45\mathrm{mL}$ centrifuge tube to ultrasonic for $15\mathrm{min}$ $^{\prime}40\mathrm{kHz},60\mathrm{W};$ . The pink products were collected by centrifuging and washing with water and ethanol. Lastly, the pink powder was obtained after drying in vacuum.
2.3. Preparation of $C o_{3}O_{4}$ -1, $C o_{3}O_{4}$ -2 and $C o_{3}O_{4}$ -3
## 2.4. Characterization and apparatus
The thermal stability of as-synthesis MOFs and MOFs-derived products was dissected by thermogravimetric (TG, Shimadzu, DTG60H). The power X-ray diffraction with Cu $\mathrm{K}_{\upalpha}$ (XRD, Philips) and the Fourier transform infrared spectra (FT-IR) spectroscopy (Thermo Inc., America) were used to qualitative analysis of products. The morphology of prepared products could be observed by means of scanning electron microscope (SEM, Hitachi, S4800). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEOL), high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM, JEOL) and selected area electron diffraction (SAED, JEOL) could investigated the sizes, exposed plane and crystal type of products. The specific surface areas of as-prepared products were obtained through the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET, Micromeritics, 3Flex 3.01) nitrogen adsorption-desorption. CTL-based sensor apparatus as the same as the previous works of our group that mainly composed of three sections: a reaction cell used to generate signals, a BPCL Ultra-weak Luminescence Analyzer (Biophysics Institute of Chinese Academy of Science, Beijing, China) captured the signals that emitted from reaction cell, and a computer (Lenovo) read data.
## 2.5. Cataluminescence sensing measurements
The CTL sensing process of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}–1$ in response to $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ was described as follow. More simply, about $0.03\mathrm{g}\mathrm{Co}_{3}0_{4}.1$ were coated on a heated ceramic rod which was inserted in a quartz tube with $10\mathrm{mm}$ inner diameter and length of $10\mathrm{cm}$ . The working temperature $(241^{\circ}\mathsf C)$ was controlled by a voltage controller. Air was used as carrier gas and the flow rate $(0.6\mathrm{L}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ ) was monitored by a precision flow meter. The $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ gas was brought into the quartz tube and was catalyzed oxidation by $0_{2}$ on the surface of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}–1$ with the CTL emission. Meanwhile, the CTL signals can be captured by a BPCL Ultra-weak luminescence analyzer.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization
To confirm the calcined products, the XRD patterns of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}–1$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -2 and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -3 were showed in Fig. 3a. The main diffraction peaks of three materials could be well assigned to the (111), (220), (311), (222), (400), (422), (511) and (440) facets of cubic spinel-type $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (JCPDS Card No. 43–1467). And no extra diffraction peaks were observed, which suggested that high pure $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was obtained via this thermal decomposition process. FT-IR spectra was used to further verify the calcined products, Fig. 3b presented each sample had two distinct bands at $\nu$ $,(573\mathsf{c m}^{-1},$ ) and $\nu\:(667\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ ), which corresponded to the stretching vibrations of Co-O [35]. These characterization results are sufficient to show that cobalt-based MOFs generated pure cubic spinel-type $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ after thermolysis.
## 3.2. Thermal decomposition time
On the basis of the thermogravimetric behavior of ZIF-67 (Fig. 2a), $400^{\circ}\mathsf C$ was chosen as a suitable thermal decomposition temperature. ${\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}–1$ was synthesized easily by thermal decomposition of ZIF-67 at $400^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , and then at $300^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . We firstly discussed the influence of thermal decomposition time, the
XRD patterns elucidated that pyrolysis ZIF-67 at the temperature of $400^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ and $3\mathrm{h}$ , the XRD diffraction peaks can match well with cubic spinel-type $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . However, some diffraction peaks corresponding to origin ZIF-67 MOF could be seen from the red small rectangle in Fig. 4, which indicated that there were a small amount of MOFs raw materials maintaining in $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ at such a short thermal decomposition time. When time was extended to $\mathtt{4h}$ or even longer, the XRD patterns only presented the diffraction peaks of cubic spinel-type $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ without other miscellaneous diffraction peaks appeared. Considering the thermal decomposition time was too short to be fully decomposed, $\mathtt{4h}$ was chosen as the optimal thermal decomposition time.
The morphology of MOFs and MOFs-derived $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ can be observed from SEM images shown in Fig. 5. The Fig. 5a–c showed the images of dodecahedra ZIF-67, nanosheets packed Co-MOF-71 and nanoplate Co-BTC, respectively. It was well known that reasonable control of thermal decomposition conditions could easily get dodecahedra $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}–1$ , nanoparticles $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}{-}2$ as well as $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}{-}3$ . Many works have been proved that ${\sf N}_{2}$ can effectively protect the derivatives morphology [37]. As expected, the dodecahedra ZIF-67 was able to acquire the corresponding dodecahedra ${\cal C}_{{\sf0}_{3}}{\sf O}_{4}–1$ after calcining in ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmosphere but it failed to acquire the product after calcining directly in air.
To further study the morphology and size of as-synthesized three $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ products, TEM and HRTEM was conducted. Fig. 6a and d revealed that the as-synthesized dodecahedra $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -1 were monodispersed with a uniform diameter of ca. $200-250\mathrm{nm}$ , and the exposed lattice planes were $\{311\}$ facet with a lattice space of $0.24\mathrm{nm}$ and $\{220\}$ facet with a lattice space of $0.29\mathrm{nm}$ . Fig. 6b and e showed that nanoparticles $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}{-}2$ had an average diameter of ca. $40{-}50\mathrm{nm}$ , the dominant exposed planes were 112 according to a set of $\{220\}$ and a set of $\{111\}$ planes [38]. Fig. 6c and f displayed that nanoparticles ${\cal C}_{{\sf0}_{3}{\sf0}_{4}-3}$ had an average diameter of ca. $20{-}30\mathrm{nm}$ , and the lattice planes were as the same as the dodecahedra $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -1, which were $\{311\}$ and $\{220\}$ facets. The corresponding selected area electron diffraction (SAED) images in the insert of Fig. 6d–f interpreted that the as-synthesized three $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ samples had polycrystalline structure.
3.3. Discussion on CTL response of $H_{2}S$ gas on the surface of $C o_{3}O_{4}$ -1, $C o_{3}O_{4}$ -2 and $C o_{3}O_{4}$ -3
$\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ gas can be catalyzed oxidation into sulfur dioxide on the surface of catalyst at eligible temperature and catalyst in the presence of oxygen [39]. For CTL sensing, the catalytic activity of metal oxide catalysts and CTL intensity generally exist a certain correlation [40]. Hence we used the CTL intensity and signal-noise ratio $(S/\mathsf{N})$ value to evaluate the catalytic activities of as-synthesized three $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ samples. We  observed the catalytic activities of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ samples under a certain experimental conditions. As seen from Fig. 7, it was obvious that $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}–1$ had a stronger CTL intensity as well as $S/\mathsf{N}$ ratio value, and the CTL intensity was set into the order of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}{-}1>$ $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}{-}3>\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}{-}2$ . Generally, porous solid catalytic with a large surface-to-volume ration is favorable [11].
To explain the different CTL properties of these three $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ for $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ sensing, their specific surface area were measured by nitrogen adsorption-desorption and pore-size distribution were obtained by Barrett-Joyner-Halenda (BJH) calculation of adsorption branches. From Fig. 8a–c, these three $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ belonged to typical type IV curves with mesoporous feature. As seen from Fig. 8d, the specific surface areas of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}–1$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}–2$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}–3$ were $47.01\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $41.56\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $40.55\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, which were higher than that of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ reported previously [41]. And the correspondingly average size of pores were 6.0, 10.5 and $6.3\mathrm{nm}$ , following the order of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}\mathrm{-}2>\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}\mathrm{-}3>\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}\mathrm{-}1$ . The measurement results manifested that $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}–1$ possessed the highest specific surface area and the smallest size of pores, may be an evidence for the enhancement of CTL intensity. However, there is a controversy between $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}{-}2$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}{-}3$ . Many efforts have proved that the catalytic performance of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ is not only dependent on its morphology and size, but also affected by the exposed crystal facets [38,42]. Spinel-type $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ has a unique structure containing $C0^{3+}$ cations in octahedral coordination and $C o^{2+}$ cations in tetrahedral coordination [41]. In this structure, the $C0^{3+}$ cations were regarded as active sites while $C o^{2+}$ cations is inactive. Moreover, surface differential diffraction studies have proved that the active $C0^{3+}$ cations are presented only on the $\{110\}$ plane [41,43,44]. From the HRTEM images in Fig. 7, the exposed facets of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}–1$ as well as ${\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}-$ 3 are $\{311\}$ and $\{220\}$ , while $\{220\}$ is parallel to $\{110\}$ . When $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ go through the surface of ${C o}_{3}{0}_{4}$ , $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ prefers to react with oxygen that with active $C0^{3+}$ cations coordination. After adsorbed $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ is catalytic oxidized, active $C0^{3+}$ cations are reduced into inactive $C0^{2+}$ cations to generate oxygen vacancy. Then inactive $C o^{2+}$ cations are oxidized to active $C0^{3+}$ cations again by the $0_{2}$ in the air flow. The whole process relates to active $C0^{3+}$ cations reduction and oxidation at the $\{110\}$ plane. ${\cal C}_{{\sf0}_{3}{\sf0}_{4}-2}$ preferential exposed facet is $\{112\}$ , which possessed high catalytic activity [45]. However, active $C0^{3+}$ cations in $\{110\}$ crystal plane are rich, and the catalytic oxidation performance is more active than $\{112\}$ crystal planes [30]. Take exposed plane into account, it is reasonable that $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}{-}3$ owning $\{110\}$ crystal plane has higher catalytic oxidation performance than $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}{-}2$ . Therefore, $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}{-}3$ has stronger CTL intensity than $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}{-}2$ . And CTL signals sorted from strong to weak theoretically display $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}\mathrm{-}2\mathrm{>Co_{3}O_{4}}\mathrm{-}3\mathrm{>Co_{3}O_{4}}\mathrm{-}1$ , which are in agreement with experimental results. According to the CTL responses to $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ on the surface of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}{-}1$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}{-}2$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ - 3, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -1 was chosen as the suitable sensing material for $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ detection.
## 3.4. Optimization of CTL sensor
To acquire high CTL performance of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -1 as sensing material, CTL test conditions were optimized. The operating wavelength of CTL emission was firstly investigated by using seven optical filters such as 400, 425, 440, 460, 490, 535 and $555\mathrm{nm}$ . As shown in Fig. 9a, the CTL intensity as the same as the $S/\mathsf{N}$ value reached at maximum with $400\mathrm{nm}$ optical filter.
As a major parameter, temperature would play a significant role on CTL performance. Because the rate of the catalytic reaction is dependent on reaction temperature. It can be concluded from Fig. 9b, the maximum CTL intensity can be acquired when the temperature reached to $241^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . Consequently, $241^{\circ}\mathsf C$ was chosen as the favorable working temperature for $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ gas-sensing.
The air flow rate has a significant effect on the CTL intensity because high rate of air flow would dilute the concentration of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ , resulting in the increasement of detection limit and resourcewasting. On the contrary low rate of air flow resulted in high background signal. As seen from Fig. 9c, the strongest CTL intensity was observed when air flow rate was $0.6\mathrm{L}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ .
## 3.5. Analytic characteristics
Analytical characteristics of cataluminescent $\mathsf{H}_{2}S$ sensor based on dodecahedra $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}–1$ was investigated under optimal experimental conditions. Gas is unstable, and fast response is one of the necessary conditions to detect gas especially in a dynamic process. Fig. 10a signified that there was similar dynamic CTL response curves of dodecahedra ${\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}.$ 1 with three different concentrations of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ at the same conditions. The response time and recovery time of the dodecahedra $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4^{-1}}$ 1 sensor were 0.1 s as well as the recovery time were $0.2s$ , respectively, which were better than most of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ gas sensor materials that had been reported previously [18–20]. It may be ascribed to the following two reasons. On one hand, dodecahedra $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -1 had large surface-to-volume ratio that could provide more charge-transfer channels and faster transfer speed. On the other hand, exposed $\{110\}$ plane possessed a number of $C0^{3+}$ cations which were active sites of catalytic oxidation of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ . The synergistic effect contributed to the enhancement of catalytic activity of dodecahedra ${\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}–1$ , thereby increasing the reaction rate. To study the stability of dodecahedra $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4^{-}}$ 1, five parallel experiments were carried out with the same concentration $16.5\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ ) of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ (Fig. 10b). The relative standard deviations $\left(\mathtt{n}=5\right)$ of the CTL intensity were less than $3\%$ , which indicated that the MOF-derived dodecahedra $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -1 sensor had the advantages in good reproducibility and high stability. Meanwhile, we have investigated the relationship between different concentration of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ and CTL intensity shown in Fig. 11. A good linear relation was presented in the concentration range of $3.3\mathrm{-}27.5\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ and the linear equation was $I{=}758.7c{-}783.5$ coupled with the correlation coefficient $r{=}0.996$ , in which the I represented the average of CTL intensity after three parallel experiments and c represented the concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\left(\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\right)$ . The detection limit was $1\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ by means of $S/N=3$ . All above proved that MOF-derived dodecahedra $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -1 was an eligible cataluminecence sensing material for $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ detection.
## 3.6. Selectivity of dodecahedra $C o_{3}O_{4}$ -1 for $H_{2}S$ sensor
Selectivity plays an important role in the field of gas sensing particularly for practical application. Similarly, under the optimal working conditions, the anti-interference feature of dodecahedra ${\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}–1$ for $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ sensor was investigated by a succession of common chemical substances including methanol, formic acid, formaldehyde, ethanol, acetic acid, acetaldehyde, carbon disulfide, propionic acid, acetone, propanal, acrylic acid, n-butanol, isobutanol, tertiary butyl alcohol, butyraldehyde, n-octane, benzene and dimethylbenzene. It was noted that the concentration of all interference substances were $65.0\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , and the $\mathsf{H}_{2}S$ was $5.5\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . As seen from Fig. 12, except for carbon disulfide, these seventeen interference substances that may interfere produced no CTL response, even though the concentration was nearly 12 times higher than $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ .
## 4. Conclusions
In this work, a facile method to successfully obtain dodecahedra porous $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ as $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ CTL sensor has been achieved via thermal decomposition of ZIF-67. Owing to the largest specific surface area and exposed $\{110\}$ planes, the dodecahedra porous ${\cal C}_{{\bf0}_{3}}{\cal O}_{4}.$ 1 showed high catalytic performance for $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ sensing, with a high selective and sensitive response. Moreover, a good linear relation was acquired between CTL intensity and the concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ in a wide range. Thus, utilizing MOFs as a template to synthesize porous catalyst becomes a new breakthrough for exploring CTL sensing materials.
